26
00:01:58,720 --> 00:02:00,510
Let's start from the very basics.

27
00:02:00,520 --> 00:02:02,750
What is a mission-critical system?

28
00:02:02,750 --> 00:02:07,310
I mean, the punchy one-liner is anything that if it fails, it hurts you.

29
00:02:07,320 --> 00:02:10,830
Particularly, mission-critical is usually it hurts you fiscally.

30
00:02:10,830 --> 00:02:16,190
If we're talking about safety-critical, we're talking about systems where people could be harmed or lives are at stake.

31
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:22,030
Okay, that uh distinction between mission and safety-critical, what is the difference there?

32
00:02:22,040 --> 00:02:28,430
It is something like you're running a major web service, you may be running a major data center.

33
00:02:28,430 --> 00:02:30,710
It is critical that your base systems never fail.

34
00:02:30,710 --> 00:02:32,830
If they fail, the whole data center is down.

35
00:02:32,840 --> 00:02:34,949
None of your clients can work.

36
00:02:34,949 --> 00:02:40,270
That can have repercussions in, for example, a whole hospital system may go down.

37
00:02:40,270 --> 00:02:43,790
Like, this is often called mission-critical, but because something like,

38
00:02:43,790 --> 00:02:51,230
for example, an AWS data center is nowadays so part of everything in our lives,

39
00:02:51,230 --> 00:02:59,310
this can have repercussions down the line where safety-critical systems fail because they haven't anticipated that case, and people could actually be harmed.

40
00:02:59,310 --> 00:03:03,750
So, the distinction is actually, from my perspective, getting harder and harder.

41
00:03:03,760 --> 00:03:15,470
The perspective that I'm taking is there's a newfound desire in software correctness for multiple reasons coming out of different directions.

42
00:03:15,470 --> 00:03:23,030
Rust as a programming language that says "Safe and correct code" directly in its claim comes out of a web browser company.

43
00:03:23,030 --> 00:03:25,270
It was originally designed at Mozilla.

44
00:03:25,270 --> 00:03:33,949
And not just because someone wanted to build such a language just for fun in Mozilla research, but because they had a legitimate need for that.

45
00:03:33,949 --> 00:03:47,111
So, that's pretty interesting that a language that we're currently talking about or like, "Is this the new language in safety-critical?" comes out of a space that is pretty far from safety-critical, but mindset-wise is pretty close. Mhm.

46
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:53,870
Yeah, and I think that sometimes we realize that the systems are safety-critical when something bad happens.

47
00:03:53,870 --> 00:03:58,590
You know, like cloud providers that have some kind of loss of service for whatever reason.

48
00:03:58,590 --> 00:04:00,990
Or I think some time ago happened even to Google.

49
00:04:00,990 --> 00:04:03,710
I think Gmail issues, things.

50
00:04:03,710 --> 00:04:09,310
So, and all of a sudden entire businesses cannot really work anymore. Yeah, that was a memory safety issue.

51
00:04:09,310 --> 00:04:13,470
It was a null pointer dereference that they actually actively ran into.

52
00:04:13,470 --> 00:04:15,151
The retro about that is quite clear about this. So.

53
00:04:15,160 --> 00:04:19,990
Yeah, you are smiling because this is like it wouldn't have happened with the rust, I guess.

54
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,030
I'm joking.

55
00:04:25,390 --> 00:04:29,390
In a way, yes. There's other bugs of that scale that you can build in rust, but rust is there to help with that.

56
00:04:29,390 --> 00:04:34,750
So, I think one of the things that legitimizes I don't need to legitimize rust,

57
00:04:34,750 --> 00:04:42,190
but that is interesting about this whole move is rust is not alone in being a new memory safe systems programming language.

58
00:04:42,190 --> 00:04:47,750
For example, Apple has developed Swift, which is pretty much in the same generation of programming languages.

59
00:04:47,750 --> 00:04:51,430
So, it's not just rust coming up and saying, "You are bad here." We know it better.

60
00:04:51,430 --> 00:04:59,470
It is much more a generation of new software development that also then invests into new base tooling because it has new needs.

61
00:04:59,470 --> 00:05:01,550
Yeah, yeah.

62
00:05:01,550 --> 00:05:08,909
Can I ask you, what are the criteria to decide that a mission or a safety critical system is actually good enough for its purpose?

63
00:05:08,919 --> 00:05:21,190
This is one of the things where the safety critical community has an edge because it has standards and documents and aside from these documents, forums where this is constantly being discussed.

64
00:05:21,190 --> 00:05:26,950
And that is something that we don't really see in, for example, like before I had fair systems, I was actually in data center operations.

65
00:05:26,950 --> 00:05:28,710
So, I have quite a bit of insight there.

66
00:05:28,710 --> 00:05:41,030
So, while there are background groups that talk about these issues, there's nothing as structured as here's an industry consortium that wants to fix these and these issues and talk about how our programming practice looks like.

67
00:05:41,030 --> 00:05:52,590
So, things like Misra, things like all the standards bodies in the ISO that decide about the IEC 61508 for industry or the ISO 26262 for automotive.

68
00:05:52,590 --> 00:06:06,870
Like all these forums or the DO-178 for avionics where you have written down standard engineering practice of what is expected and what is recommended that just doesn't exist in that mission critical space.

69
00:06:06,870 --> 00:06:12,150
So, I think a little bit there the label helps if it's safety critical or something where one of those standards is in play.

70
00:06:12,150 --> 00:06:19,630
So, can you give us an example, you know, a few things, you know, some of these criteria or some examples of what these documents specify?

71
00:06:19,630 --> 00:06:21,590
Just a small one to give people an idea of what we look at.

72
00:06:21,600 --> 00:06:29,070
The one I love is that the ISO 26262, for example, says practice on all levels of safety.

73
00:06:29,070 --> 00:06:34,430
So, it has these ASIL A, B, C, D for different levels of toughness essentially.

74
00:06:34,430 --> 00:06:38,351
It says, "If available, use a statically typed programming language." That is one of the criteria.

75
00:06:38,360 --> 00:06:42,390
And it's actually highly recommended on all levels.

76
00:06:42,390 --> 00:06:54,430
Which I sometimes use as a joke when people ask like, "Should we use Rust in automotive?" And I say, "Yes, your standard says so." But there's other things like, for example, practice restricted use of pointers.

77
00:06:54,430 --> 00:06:56,270
It says like, "Don't just hand pointers around everywhere.

78
00:06:56,270 --> 00:07:00,310
If you can pass things by value, pass them by value because that's easier."

79
00:07:00,310 --> 00:07:12,150
That's one of those things that could be in a standard or even on certain levels to assess the quality of your test suite, please use code coverage of different kinds of forms.

80
00:07:12,150 --> 00:07:14,870
Like, for example, please use statement coverage.

81
00:07:14,870 --> 00:07:20,790
Is, for example, the one that the automotive standard says for ASIL B, so roughly mid-level.

82
00:07:20,790 --> 00:07:26,470
While it says for ASIL D, use multiple condition and decision coverage, MCDC.

83
00:07:26,480 --> 00:07:28,230
So, those are things that it mandates.

84
00:07:28,240 --> 00:07:32,990
So, it mandates and recommends activities.

85
00:07:32,990 --> 00:07:40,950
One important thing is that all of those activities can be argued at a level where you say, "I do this activity.

86
00:07:40,950 --> 00:07:51,110
I don't do activity A, but I don't do activity B because I can give you an argument why in my software it actually doesn't improve quality that much and will actually focus on making the first one really thorough.

87
00:07:51,110 --> 00:07:54,630
That is a debate you then have to have with your assessor.

88
00:07:54,630 --> 00:08:04,230
And this is the other practice where I would differentiate safety-critical from mission-critical, where this practice of your software is being assessed by an independent party is much more structured.

89
00:08:04,230 --> 00:08:15,950
While in mission-critical, particularly in cybersecurity, it is just I'm doing air quotes here good practice, but it's also So, for example, we do work on cryptographic code.

90
00:08:15,950 --> 00:08:20,550
We have worked, for example, on Rust TLS, which means we will never assess this code.

91
00:08:20,550 --> 00:08:22,350
We will never claim that this code is good.

92
00:08:22,350 --> 00:08:25,430
That is for a third party to assess, and I think this is good practice.

93
00:08:25,430 --> 00:08:35,070
This interplay between the implementer is not the validator, I think is And that's completely independent of programming languages.

94
00:08:35,070 --> 00:08:44,431
So, do I understand correctly that this basically a set of recommendations at various levels, even to the nitty-gritty details of everyday coding, up to that? Yeah.

95
00:08:44,431 --> 00:08:53,310
And then any decisions that are taken maybe outside the recommendations, what you do are actually explicitly and documented decisions, so it's not by random chance that things happen.

96
00:08:53,320 --> 00:08:55,630
Exactly.

97
00:08:55,630 --> 00:08:57,230
And it's totally fine to do things that are outside of the standard.

98
00:08:57,240 --> 00:09:04,150
You can still go and say, we have an activity that is not covered in the standard, but we still think it increases software quality.

99
00:09:04,150 --> 00:09:10,670
In that case, that's a longer argument to make because you have to say it is of utility, but that's stuff.

100
00:09:10,670 --> 00:09:14,110
Like, the standards are not prescriptive.

101
00:09:14,110 --> 00:09:16,030
They are not saying like you should do this.

102
00:09:16,030 --> 00:09:22,750
The good thing I find about the safety standards is that they say, this is a very good recommendation of what you should do.

103
00:09:22,750 --> 00:09:28,350
If you're diverging from it, we need to have a conversation and someone needs to trust that your divergence is good and makes sense.

104
00:09:28,350 --> 00:09:32,910
And then how do you decide what to apply and what to apply? So, I'll give you an example.

105
00:09:32,910 --> 00:09:37,831
So, in a safety-critical system, of course, means that lives might be at risk. Mhm.

106
00:09:37,831 --> 00:09:43,710
How do you decide it could be acceptable to risk some lives in some circumstances?

107
00:09:43,710 --> 00:09:45,670
So, how is this, you know, the criteria?

108
00:09:45,670 --> 00:09:56,070
So, it's just try to understand, you know, when people go down and say, "Okay, according to what you have done, this is a good system." Because of course you cannot really eliminate the risk entirely.

109
00:09:56,080 --> 00:10:05,590
Yeah. This is a little bit out of my depth because I am very much on the front of the chain in providing the tools for this.

110
00:10:05,590 --> 00:10:07,550
I've never been in the position where I would have to argue that.

111
00:10:07,550 --> 00:10:17,230
I know that these assessments are being made and I know that for example the avionics standards are so strict because disaster is usually pretty big.

112
00:10:17,230 --> 00:10:25,310
While if you're talking about a car, that that level I can speak like that just the system and the system complexity is just a completely different category.

113
00:10:25,320 --> 00:10:27,550
Okay.

114
00:10:27,550 --> 00:10:29,870
If a plane falls from the sky, it's a massive massive disaster.

115
00:10:29,880 --> 00:10:39,750
Automotive has the problem that billions and billions of cars drive every day and they want to drive numbers down and accidents happen daily.

116
00:10:39,750 --> 00:10:42,230
That's a fact of life.

117
00:10:42,230 --> 00:10:45,870
But nowadays we're talking in Germany about thousands of accidents a year.

118
00:10:45,870 --> 00:10:50,390
There were times where we were talking about 10,000s of accidents a year.

119
00:10:50,390 --> 00:10:58,150
So, bringing that number down and further and further and further and making sure that to also mechanical and ways and it's not just about the software.

120
00:10:58,150 --> 00:11:00,190
It's also how the car is built.

121
00:11:00,190 --> 00:11:02,351
It's about the system, the entire system. You can't escape the system.

122
00:11:02,351 --> 00:11:11,990
I have I had a very nice conversation at the Safety Critical Club the last time I was there where someone said, "Safety in the end is about dealing with what's real."

123
00:11:11,990 --> 00:11:24,310
Like you can't escape reality and you usually have a physical system in place and the reality is car accidents exist, but there's a lot of people out there both on the software side and the hardware side to make sure that people don't get harmed in them.

124
00:11:24,320 --> 00:11:26,910
Yeah.

125
00:11:26,910 --> 00:11:31,471
Now, why do the tool chains for the critical systems need to be certified? What is the reason for that?

126
00:11:31,480 --> 00:11:39,630
So, the general term is being qualified because the tool chain doesn't doesn't end up like your compiler doesn't run on an engine.

127
00:11:39,630 --> 00:11:41,430
The compiler runs in your build system.

128
00:11:41,440 --> 00:11:46,310
Quite simply because compiler bugs can immediately lead to program misbehavior.

129
00:11:46,320 --> 00:11:51,750
So, they're in the direct chain of the coder.

130
00:11:51,750 --> 00:11:57,870
I write something into my text editor and this is the program that runs. And a lot of bugs are pretty much immediately obvious.

131
00:11:57,870 --> 00:12:00,472
That's the good ones. I run my tests. This is buggy.

132
00:12:00,480 --> 00:12:06,190
That's fine. And then the program never gets deployed.

133
00:12:06,190 --> 00:12:16,710
The problem is compilers have the power to generate our code and that also means any bugs in the code generation can lead to hard to trace down bugs that come down the line that may be triggered later on.

134
00:12:16,710 --> 00:12:21,030
And this is why we assess tool chains.

135
00:12:21,030 --> 00:12:33,990
So, the joking summary if I have to explain ferrocene and the whole subject of compiler qualification to people in the Rust project who are away from safety critical is they're saying like, "What are you doing? The Rust compiler is already high quality."

136
00:12:33,990 --> 00:12:37,430
And I'm saying like, "Yeah, but what's your argument that it is high quality?

137
00:12:37,430 --> 00:12:50,030
Is it we haven't found bugs for a couple of weeks or is it we are sure that for example, this feature has an appropriate amount of tests that we are reasonably sure that it is well tested." And that's basically the qualification work.

138
00:12:50,030 --> 00:12:53,270
You say Mhm.

139
00:12:53,270 --> 00:12:55,470
the tool has the following feature.

140
00:12:55,470 --> 00:13:06,630
For example, it compiles a main function and if you put in print that out hello, the output is a certain kind of binary program on a certain kind of architecture that outputs hello world.

141
00:13:06,640 --> 00:13:12,430
Pretty trivial example, but we make sure that this compilation process works well all over over time.

142
00:13:12,440 --> 00:13:23,271
Mhm. And so, when you have this qualification process, is it done for a specific version of the compiler on a specific operating system on a specific architecture? Yes.

143
00:13:23,271 --> 00:13:30,470
Are all versions kind of locked down for the qualification process or there is a bit of flexibility there.

144
00:13:30,470 --> 00:13:36,030
So, we generally release every 3 months, so every quarter.

145
00:13:36,030 --> 00:13:41,510
And we release an appropriate Rust compiler version that is currently new.

146
00:13:41,510 --> 00:13:46,830
We take a few 1 to 2 months to further validate the compiler downstream.

147
00:13:46,830 --> 00:13:52,990
That means validating it on, as you say, we need to validate it on all the targets that are supported that the main Rust project does not support.

148
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,470
So, we're running the tests there.

149
00:13:55,470 --> 00:14:05,990
We're seeing if exactly that thing is like we have for the whole Ferrous Systems project, we have written a language specification for the Rust language, so that we can say, "This is a feature of the language.

150
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:18,191
These are the tests for it." This specification, by the way, has recently been accepted off the Rust project as the Rust specification, which means the argument, "Rust doesn't have a specification." now goes away.

151
00:14:18,191 --> 00:14:20,870
That was a decade-long argument that we had.

152
00:14:20,880 --> 00:14:23,710
Rust now has a specification.

153
00:14:23,710 --> 00:14:31,231
So, a major trunk of that work is making sure that every piece in that specification is well tested. That's about 1/2.

154
00:14:31,231 --> 00:14:37,430
And the reason why we do this is to make sure that engineers that use the tool,

155
00:14:37,440 --> 00:14:47,750
and they see a tool behavior, they can go to the specification and say, "Does this behave like it's written down?" And then they look at this tool behavior, and maybe they say, "Okay.

156
00:14:47,760 --> 00:14:59,031
Okay, this is how the tool should behave. That's fine." Or they see, "Okay, something's off." And there's sometimes the discussion that people make is like, "Okay, so who's right? Who's wrong?

157
00:14:59,031 --> 00:15:02,790
Is the spec right? Is the compiler wrong?" That's actually not the interesting bit.

158
00:15:02,790 --> 00:15:06,790
If those two things disagree, you should look at what actually happens there.

159
00:15:06,790 --> 00:15:12,751
And then, this is more like the starting point for further research, because you probably found something. Mhm.

160
00:15:12,760 --> 00:15:22,550
So, coming back to my communication piece, there's always what we do is we take the gut feeling that the the compiler is good quality,

161
00:15:22,550 --> 00:15:27,790
and give you an actual argument and evidence that the Rust compiler is good quality.

162
00:15:27,790 --> 00:15:29,790
Because things can feel as good as I want.

163
00:15:29,790 --> 00:15:33,750
I don't want to be in a car where someone said, "It feels good.

164
00:15:33,750 --> 00:15:38,830
The brake feels good. It hopefully works." No, the brake has to work.

165
00:15:38,840 --> 00:15:41,310
Okay.

166
00:15:41,310 --> 00:15:45,070
And what are the limitations of this certification or qualification process?

167
00:15:45,070 --> 00:15:48,550
Because I guess cannot guarantee 100% safety.

168
00:15:48,550 --> 00:15:51,430
So, there must be some limitations or Quite a few.

169
00:15:51,430 --> 00:15:56,070
Can I just add one more thing to the activity stream and then answer that question?

170
00:15:56,080 --> 00:16:02,790
The other thing is the Rust compiler has quite a lot of features that, for example, may lead to miscompilation.

171
00:16:02,790 --> 00:16:08,670
So, for example, it has a developer convenience feature that is called incremental compilation.

172
00:16:08,670 --> 00:16:15,190
So, it compiles parts of the code and then if you recompile, it does only recompile the things that's been changed.

173
00:16:15,190 --> 00:16:24,950
This one is generally solid, but if you're using that, it may in very rare cases, because it's a very complex system, introduce bugs.

174
00:16:24,950 --> 00:16:30,630
So, what we also do is inform our customers on the final build, on the final software, please don't use this feature.

175
00:16:30,630 --> 00:16:36,350
It's generally okay, but please not in safety-critical software, because even the off chance that this triggers a bug, something.

176
00:16:36,350 --> 00:16:44,310
So, it's also a lot of customer information of writing down information that is running around the community.

177
00:16:44,310 --> 00:16:46,390
So, a lot of experienced Rust programmers know that.

178
00:16:46,390 --> 00:16:50,630
The problem is our customers don't always employ people who built the compiler.

179
00:16:50,640 --> 00:16:55,550
Uh more often than not, you know what I mean?

180
00:16:55,550 --> 00:17:04,950
So, that is also like this interesting because it's not a feature of the language itself, but it is about the tool chains available and how we use them. Pretty much.

181
00:17:04,950 --> 00:17:10,510
And so, this is one of the recommendations of using the tool chains in a particular way to be safer, I guess.

182
00:17:10,520 --> 00:17:14,230
Exactly. On your question about limits.

183
00:17:14,240 --> 00:17:16,510
The limitations.

184
00:17:16,510 --> 00:17:22,150
So, we have qualified that was first in was a bit of a research project for customer on the customer request.

185
00:17:22,150 --> 00:17:24,270
We have actually qualified the whole language.

186
00:17:24,270 --> 00:17:29,350
So, this from the tool perspective, you can use all of Rust with the tool.

187
00:17:29,360 --> 00:17:35,030
The problem is Rust has, for example, features.

188
00:17:35,030 --> 00:17:39,030
It has a specific thing called procedural macros, which are tiny annotations.

189
00:17:39,030 --> 00:17:46,870
You can put them on top of structures, and then this is actually allowed to pick this up the structure up and generate a additional code.

190
00:17:46,870 --> 00:17:54,790
And the way this works is these procedural macros are actually code libraries you can, well, either download from the internet or write yourself.

191
00:17:54,790 --> 00:18:07,190
And this is where it gets a little bit ironic where people say it's like, "Can we use proc macros in our project?" And I'm like, "Yes, from me as a tool vendor, I can tell you our proc macro interface works.

