Am I correct? Exactly, exactly.

566
00:55:35,231 --> 00:55:40,790
And I would even put that up to about 60 to 70% of what an engineer can do.

567
00:55:40,790 --> 00:55:42,870
And coming back to that question about the introduction,

568
00:55:42,880 --> 00:55:56,550
the engineers who are solid about those assessments are the best ones to introduce that new tool in your organization because what you want is an experienced engineer that tells you, "Okay, these 70% we haven't solved, but we got way better on the software side."

569
00:55:56,560 --> 00:56:00,630
And depending how much of their work is like that may differ.

570
00:56:00,630 --> 00:56:05,910
We also have a lot of people that work mostly on the software side, so that calculation is a little bit different.

571
00:56:05,920 --> 00:56:08,430
Okay.

572
00:56:08,430 --> 00:56:14,070
And another question that is both maybe interesting for companies but also the developers themselves,

573
00:56:14,070 --> 00:56:21,150
you know, how big is the job market for Rust developers in this safety-critical domain?

574
00:56:21,150 --> 00:56:27,790
Because I guess for a company is important because of course they want to have skills available if they need to hire people.

575
00:56:27,790 --> 00:56:32,350
And for developers is important because they hear, "Well, you know, with C I find a lot of jobs. I really like Rust.

576
00:56:32,350 --> 00:56:39,671
I want to move to Rust, but you know, if the market is not big enough, could be a problem to find a new job." It is growing and contracting.

577
00:56:39,680 --> 00:56:48,150
Like sometimes there's just a lot of people that want to start doing Rust nowadays, then some companies grab them up and so on so forth.

578
00:56:48,150 --> 00:56:56,470
But in general, so the Rust market, first of all, it's a little bit hard to assess because it's a new market and there's not a lot of tracking.

579
00:56:56,470 --> 00:57:00,430
There's Rust jobs available at all major safety-critical companies nowadays.

580
00:57:00,430 --> 00:57:08,630
I've seen them very often in the Q&amp;M departments, like just the quality management side, because that's usually where they start, but they haven't interested in moving in.

581
00:57:08,630 --> 00:57:17,630
I've seen quite a few people actually leave their job taking a pay cut and going somewhere else just so that they could do Rust.

582
00:57:17,630 --> 00:57:25,910
So it's also a thing that experienced engineers see that as their next career step, and that's a way you can snatch them up if you have a project to offer.

583
00:57:25,910 --> 00:57:28,310
That's something that we've seen.

584
00:57:28,310 --> 00:57:36,870
The biggest mistake that we've seen is actually in hiring is a quite trivial one is expecting too much Rust experience of potential hires.

585
00:57:36,870 --> 00:57:47,750
Because I know people in the Rust community who are extremely knowledgeable in the language, extremely knowledgeable in putting in of projects, but who have never worked Rust on a job because they are C++ developers somewhere.

586
00:57:47,760 --> 00:57:58,270
Yeah, okay. And they often fall through screening because it says not 5 years of professional Rust experience, but they have 30 years of professional C++ programming experience.

587
00:57:58,280 --> 00:58:02,030
this is one of the unfortunate things with all development jobs.

588
00:58:02,030 --> 00:58:11,470
Often companies look for specific language stuff, when in fact experienced programmers can pick up the specific skills very, very quickly.

589
00:58:11,480 --> 00:58:18,990
But the job market, I think, is mature and my indicators for a mature market is people move jobs.

590
00:58:18,990 --> 00:58:21,310
Like people move away from Rust jobs into Rust jobs.

591
00:58:21,310 --> 00:58:25,150
I think that's my most important indicator that's that's employee mobility.

592
00:58:25,160 --> 00:58:28,070
Yeah.

593
00:58:28,070 --> 00:58:34,070
There's specialized recruiters actually just doing Rust recruiting, which also speaks a little bit to maturity.

594
00:58:34,070 --> 00:58:41,590
And most companies that I know that post their jobs at the right places report, "Yeah, we're finding about 10 to 20 good applicants."

595
00:58:41,590 --> 00:58:53,510
The other thing is usually Rust jobs, and that's something that I hear from a lot of people that are hiring and also seeing in our own hiring data, is the medium level of quality of the people who actually apply is pretty high.

596
00:58:53,510 --> 00:59:00,831
And who's complaining about I have 10 to 20 pretty good pre-screened candidates rather than I have 200 with Yeah. who would spot you.

597
00:59:00,840 --> 00:59:11,272
So actually, so if they they have to screen fewer candidates, but they have a better chance to actually hire someone because the average level is higher anyway. Yes. Okay.

598
00:59:11,272 --> 00:59:12,990
And so my last question is about the future.

599
00:59:12,990 --> 00:59:18,430
How do you envision the future of Rust in the safety-critical domain?

600
00:59:18,440 --> 00:59:22,470
Okay, I envision it pretty big.

601
00:59:24,790 --> 00:59:26,870
I honestly think Rust will become the next standard language for those areas.

602
00:59:26,880 --> 00:59:34,470
And the reason for that is memory safety will become a mandate in a lot of projects, and we're already seeing that.

603
00:59:34,480 --> 00:59:44,070
We're seeing requests for quotations being handed around that say, "Do only implement in a memory safe programming language." So, that's the one thing.

604
00:59:44,070 --> 00:59:50,870
So, and we're seeing like the buying side is far more interested in you need to have a memory safety story.

605
00:59:50,870 --> 00:59:58,510
And we'll enter a space where, okay, I use a programming language where I need additional tooling, additional skilled engineers, and so on and so forth.

606
00:59:58,510 --> 01:00:01,351
Or I could just take one that actually fits that bill right away. Yeah.

607
01:00:01,360 --> 01:00:03,430
That is the one thing.

608
01:00:03,430 --> 01:00:11,790
The other thing I see though is there's a very good interchange between all of those languages, and C++ is still in the game, to be completely fair there.

609
01:00:11,800 --> 01:00:16,390
And they say it's like competition is business.

610
01:00:16,390 --> 01:00:18,470
So, that also applies to programming languages.

611
01:00:18,470 --> 01:00:25,750
So, it is very important that a Swift and C++ are around because that also makes sure that the Rust team is not lazy.

612
01:00:25,750 --> 01:00:30,270
And I know, for example, we have very good relationship with the C++ committee.

613
01:00:30,280 --> 01:00:44,870
Or some I wouldn't say like I I can't claim an official relationship with the C++ committee, but we've been talking for years about things, and I know everything that is done in the C++ side, they will also look at what Rust does, and we obviously look at what C++ does.

614
01:00:44,880 --> 01:00:55,870
LLVM has just announced last week that they want to have a committee in which they do safety information on the clang compiler, and put that open source, very similar to how we do.

615
01:00:55,870 --> 01:00:59,150
There is a good cross-pollination between the two sides.

616
01:00:59,160 --> 01:01:02,870
As they say in Germany, there's music in it.

617
01:01:02,880 --> 01:01:11,230
And this is much more the space where I want to be, where people go and meet and say like, "Okay, we have three technologies here, and let's figure out how we make it better and better."

618
01:01:11,240 --> 01:01:19,551
Sounds like a situation where there is a healthy competition in terms of trying to improve all these various languages and technologies. Yeah, exactly.

619
01:01:19,551 --> 01:01:22,230
And it's important.

620
01:01:22,230 --> 01:01:28,350
Like, we have literally millions and millions of lines of C and C++ code out there.

621
01:01:28,350 --> 01:01:30,830
It would be bad if we ended up saying, "Okay, this is done.

622
01:01:30,840 --> 01:01:43,390
No one cares about this." Like we This is just structurally impossible, but it also means for a language like Rust going in and saying, "Okay, C is now how much from the '60s?"

623
01:01:43,400 --> 01:01:47,830
One of the oldest languages we still use in broad use.

624
01:01:47,830 --> 01:01:50,470
C++ is a little bit younger.

625
01:01:50,470 --> 01:01:59,670
It would also be like as engineers we also need to say there's probably a lot of learning that we can take from all of these years and not recommit those mistakes and put that into a new product.

626
01:01:59,670 --> 01:02:03,230
And Rust is just to be clear, this is a debate that we very often have.

627
01:02:03,230 --> 01:02:06,390
Rust is an open source project that sees itself as a product.

628
01:02:06,400 --> 01:02:12,270
So this is something that needs to go into the hands of users and needs to be designed with that mindset.

629
01:02:12,280 --> 01:02:16,830
And I actually think it's one of the big factors in its success.

630
01:02:16,830 --> 01:02:22,030
Like aside from all of the details, that mindset is of The most important thing is the user.

631
01:02:22,040 --> 01:02:28,270
And this is something where we have quite a lot of good exchange and I think we're in a good space in terms of programming languages.

632
01:02:28,270 --> 01:02:32,110
If you love programming languages, we're in a great space.

633
01:02:32,120 --> 01:02:34,111
Okay. Thanks, Florian.

634
01:02:34,111 --> 01:02:36,230
So now we have come close to the end of the episode.

635
01:02:36,230 --> 01:02:41,390
Is there anything that we missed that you'd like to add?

636
01:02:41,390 --> 01:02:43,990
No, I think we talked about how all programming languages are great in the end.

637
01:02:43,990 --> 01:02:47,350
I think that's a good ending.

638
01:02:49,710 --> 01:02:51,590
Okay. So thank you, Florian, for coming to the show.

639
